## openshift managing deployments 

# selecting a deployment strategy
2 options to manage downtime:
- stop all instances
- roll out in batches (2 versions are running for a while at the same time) -- but there should be backward-compatibilty betw versions (mostly not a difference in storage config) 

2 strategies: recreate, rollingUpdate

btw. deploymentconfig resource -- deprecated, mostly overlaps with deployment

# rollingUpdate
- default
- deployment spec > strategy > rollingUpdate params: maxUnavailable specifies how many replicas can be unavailable during deployment 
maxSearch - how many replicas can be created above the set number of pods during re-deployment

# recreate
- all replicas are recreated at the same time 

# deployment version switching

blue-green deployment
- switching back nd forth betw versions
- editing route config allows for simple switching

a/b deployment
- gradual swiching by percentage
- alternateBackends setting in route specification
- weights for ^

# rollout
- provides the cancel, pause, undo, retry, and more options for deployments
- making several changes in one go to deployment (env changes, volume mounts)
oc rollout pause deployment/app
- config changes won't rebuild the deployment

oc rollout resume deployment/app

oc rollout  history
- revisions list

- you can annotate revisions to show up in rollout history:
oc annotate deployment/app kubernetes.io/change-cause="<user defined annotation>"

- getting back to the previous version
- interally: deployments use replicaset to control instances (replicaset stores deployment config for a particular revision):

oc rollout undo deployment/applcation # goes one rev back
oc rollout undo deployment/applcation --to-revision=5
- this takes the specified revision and makes it the last revision in history

# scale
- scales the number of replicas for a given deployment or deployment config
oc scale deployment example-deployment --replicas=3

# configmap resource
# secret resource
- storing configuration/sensitve config data
- designed to store read-only data to be shared with application
- secrets can be encrypted
- sets of key value pairs

- 3 ways of creating:
oc crete configmap mycfg --from-literal=mykey=myval --from-literal=mykey1=mykey2
oc create cm mycfg2 --from-file=myfile # filenames become keys, contents become values
oc create cm mycfg3 --from-file=mydir # all files in dir are used as configuration, filenames become keys, file contents become values

- similar with secrets
- secrets have 3 types: docker-registry, tls, generic
- types have predefined params

# sharing cm/secret with deployments
- via env vars:
oc set env deploment/app --from=cm/mycfg
- vars from cm are used in deployment, similarly for secret:
oc set env deploment/app --from=secret/mysecr

- via volume:
oc set volume deployment/app --add --secret-name=secret1 --mount-path /mysecret # mount path will be created in the pod by os
- use --cm-name to use config map

- application in the pod needs to be configured to use this configuration 

# pod security
- security context constraits
- default one:
oc describe scc 
- can be defined in the Deployment object under securityContext

- modify scc associated with a deployment
- serviceaccount can do the binding betw deployment and scc

os get scc
- many predefined ones

- create service account
oc create sa runasny
oc adm po
oc adm policy add-scc-to-user anyuid -z runasany
- adding policy to a service account
oc set serviceaccount deployment/app runasany
- binds sa with a deployment

# deploying stateful apps
- apps requiring persistent storage
- persistent volumes -- different lifecycle from the attaching pods (filesystem or block volume type)
- persistent volume claims -- represent a request for PV
oc get pvc
- dynamic provisioning -- cluster dynamically creates a PV for PVC

- adding storage to deployments
oc set volumes deploy/mydeployment ...

- statefulset resource -- pods with stable id
- not supported in oc cli yet
- need to create a spec
- ui: admim persc, workloads > statfulSets -- here you can get a template, or use chatgpt

# application health
- resource allocation
- 2 properties for an deployment:

oc set resources deployment <mydep> --limits=cpu=200m --requests=cpu=100m,memory=256Mi
- requests param influences scheduling by openshift -- it will reserve reources for app only on nodes that have enough resources 
- limits param provides ceiling for an app, m-suffix for CPU uses milicore values

- health check
oc set probe dc/myapp --startup
- checks on the start, retries if failure, if successful it never runs again
- restart actions can be tracked in os, you could get an sms

oc set probe dc/myapp --readiness
- it checks if app can serve data
- if it fails, it removes the pod from the service
- if success, the ip addr of the pod is put back to the service

oc set probe dc/myapp --liveness
- checks if the processes are alive
- if it fails, it restarts the pod

other params to probe
--get-url=URL -- probe url, e.g. http://:8080/startup -- uses localhost of the pod
--get-tcp=PORT -- probe port
-- CMD -- run a custom code that does the check
--initial-delay-seconts -- give it time to start up
--failure-treshold -- how many restarts allowed until considered failed -- crashes the entire pod -- detectable event by the alerting system, you could get an sms
--period-seconts -- how often to check for readiness and liveness probes



